Behind every growth story is a balance sheet that can sustain it. Our balance sheet has the flexibility 
and liquidity to achieve the multi-year growth we have outlined throughout this letter. 
What does that mean in practice? Our leverage is conservative, approximately 5x debt-to 
EBITDA, with an attractive 4.5% all-in borrowing cost. We have no significant debt maturities 
until 2028, eliminating any near-term refinancing pressure. And we are well-hedged against 
interest rate volatility, so rising rates are not a risk that keeps us up at night. 
On top of that, we have liquidity. In addition to our $525 million revolving credit facility, we have 
several hundred million dollars of dry powder on hand and ready to deploy. Capital that gives us 
the agility to move quickly and confidently when the right opportunity presents itself. 
As we look forward to the balance of 2026 and beyond, our balance sheet will not be the reason 
our growth story stalls. It is the reason it can continue.
...
Balance Sheet Strength and Liquidity 
In 2025, we increased total indebtedness to support acquisitions and redevelopment projects while improving the overall risk profile of our 
balance sheet. We reduced our interest rate exposure by lowering variable-rate debt, reflecting disciplined leverage management and the use of 
equity capital to fund growth. In addition, we amended our senior unsecured credit facility to reduce borrowing costs and extend maturities, 
further enhancing liquidity and financial flexibility. We have no significant REIT Portfolio debt maturit…

[10] Acadia Realty's Dual Performance: Strong Core NOI Amidst $25 Million Impairments
URL: https://www.panabee.com/news/acadia-realty-trust-earnings-q2-2025-report
However, the Investment Management segment presented a less favorable picture. Acadia recognized $24.6 million in impairment charges on two properties within Fund III and Fund IV, largely due to shortened hold periods. This suggests that certain value-add investments are not meeting original expectations or that prior valuations were overly optimistic. Further impacting the segment, a $9.6 million loss on change in control was recorded upon consolidating the Renaissance Portfolio, indicating that the previously held equity interest had a carrying value higher than its fair value at consolidation. These events contributed to a $4 million decrease in net income attributable to Acadia shareholders from this segment in the first half of 2025.
...
Strategically, Acadia has been aggressive in expanding its Core Portfolio, with year-to-date acquisitions totaling $551.5 million, largely comprising high-quality street retail in key urban corridors. The consolidation of the Renaissance Portfolio, valued at $245.7 million, further underscores a pivot towards directly owned, high-barrier-to-entry retail assets. This expansion has been supported by diverse capital sources, including the successful settlement of 11.2 million outstanding forward shares under its At-The-Market (ATM) program, generating $277.9 million in net proceeds, and drawing on new debt facilities.

Acadia's total consolidated indebtedness increased by $266.5 million to $1.81 billion, signaling a leveraged growth strategy. While the company successfully amended its senior unsecured credit facility to reduce borrowing rates by 10 basis points on the entire $925 million facility, improving future interest expense, it also faces a substantial debt maturity wall. Approximately $474 million in consolidated debt and $14…
