Real-time object detection and tracking is an important and computationally intensive task that is gaining more attention in the field of autonomous systems due to its role in enabling safe and efficient operations in applications such as autonomous vehicles, robotics, and surveillance systems. In object tracking situations such as autonomous driving, processing speed is critical to avoid danger to the driver or pedestrians. Recently, a novel object detection algorithm called RT-DETR (Real-Time DEtection TRansformer) has emerged, demonstrating superior speed compared to the popular YOLO series while maintaining competitive accuracy. In recent years, many edge devices optimized for artificial intelligence have been developed, allowing for faster tensor operations thereby leading to faster model inference. These edge devices are embedded systems that are a smaller size and have low power consumption, which allows them to operate close to where the data is generated, reducing latency. Our study, collaboratively performed with the Nonproliferation and National Security Department and Computational Science Initiative at Brookhaven National Laboratory, uses the framework of NVIDIA TensorRT to optimize models in the task of object tracking and detection on NVIDIA’s Orin edge device. Our best resulting model is the FP16 DLA model with an average inference time of 19.9416 milliseconds and throughput of 50.1465 queries (frames) per second. This is a five-fold improvement compared with the standard unoptimized Pytorch FP32 model, with practically no accuracy sacrifice. Our study shows that applying TensorRT and quantization on object tracking and detection on NVIDIA’s Orin device is effective in reducing prediction time allowing for faster detection.
