We now provide a background on YOLO evolution (v5–v8) and edge relevance. YOLO variants have made architectural changes to improve accuracy–efficiency trade-offs for in-place deployment in resource-constrained situations since 2021 [3, 8, 10, 40]. YOLOv5u added an anchor-free split head in the v5 family [41]; YOLOv6 implemented EfficientRep backbones, RepPAN aggregation, and a quantization-friendly decoupled head [42]; YOLOv7 added stretched ELAN scaling in combination with re-parameterized convolutional blocks [43]; and YOLOv8 formalized an anchor-free decoupled head in combination with a streamlined C2f backbone/neck and strong exportability in PyTorch, ONNX, and TensorRT [40, 44]. These optimizations minimize post-processing overheads and enable embedded GPU/TPU deployment, driving variant-level benchmarking to examine trade-offs across model size regimes [3, 8, 10, 26]. These architectural refinements were explicitly designed to reduce compute overhead, enable precision-friendly deployment (FP16/INT8), and make YOLO variants increasingly suitable for real-time edge benchmarks.
...
