Planning a Melbourne laundry renovation? Learn how dryer type, ventilation, cabinetry and moisture control should be resolved before the room is built.
...
A laundry can look finished and still perform badly. If heat and moisture cannot escape, the room may feel damp, cabinetry can be harder on appliances, and a vented dryer can push humid air into the house. The time to solve that is before the cabinetry, doors and services are fixed.

This guide explains the practical decisions Melbourne homeowners should make when planning a laundry renovation. It is general guidance: the exact solution should follow the selected appliance instructions and the requirements that apply to your home.

## Choose the dryer before finalising the cabinetry

Do not design a generic appliance opening and choose the dryer later. Dryer types manage moisture differently, and that changes the room.
...
A vented dryer sends hot, humid air out of the machine. The Australian Government's Energy Rating guidance says these dryers need to be vented outdoors; without that, surrounding walls can become wet. It also notes that the National Construction Code requires a shaft or duct to outside air where vented clothes dryers are installed.

If you are keeping or buying a vented dryer, plan the duct route before cabinetry is manufactured. A short, deliberate route is easier to accommodate than trying to snake a duct through completed cupboards.
...
Heat-pump and condenser dryers collect moisture rather than deliberately exhausting humid air into the room. Some use a removable water container; others can drain condensed water away. They still need the clearances, airflow and service access stated by their manufacturer.

