Unreasonable Noise 
 Under the Environmental Protection Act 2017 it is an offence to create unreasonable 
noise on a residential premises at any time of day. 
 The use of equipment that causes noise which is audible in another dwelling during 
prohibited hours is unlawful. The Noise Schedule overleaf details the type of equipment 
and the prohibited times. 
 Equipment used during allowable hours may be unreasonable depending on the volume, 
intensity, frequency, duration, time, place and other circumstances. 
 Noise can be considered a nuisance under the Public Health and Wellbeing Act 2008, 
and under this Act a person must not cause a nuisance or knowingly allow a nuisance to 
emanate from any land owned or occupied by that person. 
 Police and authorised officers of Council can investigate noise issues, enter premises, 
issue directions to abate noise, and issue Infringement Notices for failure to comply. 
Penalties for unreasonable noise or breaching a direction range with maximum penalties 
of up to 120 penalty units (for an individual) or 600 penalty units (for a body corporate) if 
prosecuted. 
