LONDON, 14th April 2025 – Quantum industry professionals are optimistic that quantum utility will be achieved within the next decade, research from Economist Impact today reveals.

According to the study, which surveyed quantum professionals across the UK, Europe, North America and Asia, 83 percent say quantum utility – when quantum computers will overcome hardware and error correction challenges to perform better than classical computers – will be realised within 10 years or fewer.

Of this figure, one third believe it will be reached within one to five years. Just 3 percent of respondents believe quantum utility remains more than 30 years away, countering claims made – and then rescinded – from NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang earlier this year, Economist Impact said.

However, despite this optimism, challenges in technical development, talent acquisition, and executive-level understanding persist, which must be overcome to support quantum’s development. The research – which aims to provide a benchmark for distinguishing between quantum hype and reality – comes ahead of the Economist Impact’s upcoming 4th annual Commercialising Quantum Global summit in London on May 13-14, which will explore these issues in more depth.

The survey highlights three major challenges facing quantum advantage – the moment when quantum technologies will be ready to implement into business end-users’ daily operations:

- 75 percent say lack of understanding at board level is holding back adoption
- 75 percent identify a shortage of talent and expertise as a critical issue
- 82 percent cite overcoming technical challenges, particularly error correction, as a key hurdle

