The 2024 baseline documented severe, system-wide gaps: only 2% of HPs used the standardized MCH card; 4% managed high-risk mothers on labour; 0% had an effective blood transfusion mechanism; 7% offered MR services seven days a week; 9% had PMTCT guidelines in place; and only 7% met the minimum midwife staffing threshold. These were not peripheral shortcomings but fundamental failures in the foundational standards of safe delivery care. The 2025 follow-up data reveal selective but significant improvements alongside persistent and newly emerged gaps. Standardized MCH card use surged from 2% to 87% at HPs (+85pp) and from 14% to 89% at PHCCs (+75pp), while partograph adherence at PHCCs rose from 74% to 92% (+18pp). First 24-hour postpartum observation was ensured by 18% of HPs and 84% of PHCCs in 2025. Conversely, nutrition centre integration at PHCCs fell from 60% to 39% (21pp), community outreach at HPs from 67% to 47% (20pp), and FP seven-day service availability at PHCCs from 80% to 63% (-17pp) — declines plausibly driven by health worker turnover and implementing partner transitions. Against a backdrop of 46 maternal deaths and 491 perinatal deaths in 2024 — with a maternal mortality ratio estimated at 295 per 100,000 live births in 2023, and 67% of community maternal deaths occurring in women who delivered at home (78% of whom had attended four or more ANC visits) — these persistent structural gaps carry direct, life-or-death implications.
