Around more than half of the refugees living in fragile humanitarian settings worldwide are women and girls of reproductive age who are the most vulnerable when pregnant. 1 However, they continue to be at risk of poor pregnancy outcomes owing to the disparities in the use of maternal and reproductive health services, affecting long-term maternal morbidity and mortality, 2 3 in addi tion to psychological and physical strain from displace ment and resettlement. 4 In general, most maternal deaths occur due to preventable pregnancy-related complications. Therefore, delay to seek care is a signif icant contributing factor to fatalities in low-income countries, as established in the literature. 5 According to the ‘three delay model’ conceptualised by Thad deus and Maine, the delay associated with maternal mortality occurs at three different stages. 6 The first delay is the delay in deciding to seek care; the second delay is in reaching the health facility; the third and final delay occurs in receiving quality care.

Notably, the three delays are more likely to occur during the COVID-19 pandemic for reasons including a hesitancy to seek care due to fear of infection, limited access to transportation because of movement restric tions and lockdown, and reduced availability of obstet rical facilities. 7 Past epidemics such as Ebola have revealed a drastic reduction in facility utilisation for antenatal visits, delivery and po…

