How did vTv Therapeutics’ R&D and G&A expenses change in 2025? 

 In 2025, research and development expenses increased to $17.9 million from $11.5 million, driven by higher cadisegliatin clinical spending and a Novo license milestone. General and administrative expenses rose to $14.9 million from $13.7 million, mainly from higher share-based compensation, payroll, and legal costs. 

 What is cadisegliatin and how is it being developed by vTv Therapeutics? 

 Cadisegliatin (TTP399) is a liver-selective glucokinase activator being investigated as a first-in-class oral adjunctive treatment for type 1 diabetes. It has FDA Breakthrough Therapy designation and is currently in a U.S. Phase 3 trial, with additional Phase 2 work planned in type 2 diabetes. 

