If you are currently using a household combination refrigerator/freezer, we strongly recommend you upgrade to a biomedical-grade unit. If upgrade isn’t possible, it is necessary to purchase a separate freezer and only using the main section of the household refrigerator. According to studies conducted by National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST), household combination units are less capable of maintaining proper storage temperatures in both the refrigerator and freezer compartments. This is because cold air from the freezer blows directly into the refrigerator compartment and onto the sensitive vaccine. The best practice is to choose a separate refrigerator and freezer purpose-built for the precise storage of vaccines. Additionally, the freezer compartment should not be used to store personal food and drink as it results in frequent door opening and temperature destabilization. As of July 1, 2024, Hawaii VFC providers are not allowed to use the freezer compartment of a household combination unit and are required to purchase a separate stand-alone freezer unit.
