URL: https://labsup.net/blogs/blog/nsf-ansi-456-for-vaccine-storage
The NSF/ANSI 456 Standard for Vaccine Storage establishes the essential criteria for the design and performance of storage units used by those who administer vaccines. This standard pertains to vaccine storage refrigerators, freezers, and combination units. The Centers for Disease Control (CDC) Toolkit continues to be the benchmark for the safe storage and handling of vaccines. The new standard does not replace the CDC's Vaccine Storage and Handling Toolkit guidelines, which provide best practices for vaccine storage. Instead, it outlines design protocols and performance criteria specifically for the cold storage equipment referenced in the CDC's Toolkit, and the NSF 456 certification remains a voluntary standard, as no regulatory body currently mandates NSF 456 units for vaccine storage.

The standard was established by the NSF/ANSI Joint Committee on Vaccine Storage, comprising members from public health agencies such as the CDC and NIST, healthcare providers, vaccine suppliers, and equipment manufacturers. This standard sets forth comprehensive construction and performance guidelines for vaccine cold storage equipment.
...
NSF/ANSI 456 certification testing utilizes aluminum thermal ballasts, known as vaccine simulation devices (VSDs), strategically positioned inside the cabinet. These defined positions ensure temperature uniformity is met throughout the entire refrigerator or freezer regardless of where the vaccine(s) are stored. The testing protocol employs a data acquisition rate that captures temperatures 30-90 times faster than standard data loggers, to ensure accurate data. The assessment includes evaluating temperature performance in both empty and fully loaded units, during sequential 8-second door openings, as well as analyzing pulldown time following exten…

