Something exciting will be in the air at Longwood Gardens – literally – when a spectacular display of grand treehouses opens today at the country’s premier horticultural showplace. Called “Nature’s Castles: The Treehouse Reimagined,” the display is part of Longwood’s Arbor Day weekend activities and continues through Nov. 23. Throughout spring, summer and fall, visitors of all ages can explore three unique, large-scale treehouses situated in picturesque parts of Longwood. The trio of tremendous treehouses showcases the creative work of the nation’s best treehouse designers.For young visitors, the display is a chance to climb up into a treehouse and get a birds-eye view of the Gardens below and beyond, while being reconnected with nature. For older visitors, the display rekindles memories of childhood, of carefree days spent in a rustic sanctuary high up in a tree. But these treehouses are definitely not the kind to be found in any suburban backyard. They’re magnificent structures, true castles in the air that show how Longwood has taken the treehouse to new heights.The first creation, Lookout Loft, is located in Longwood’s shady Forest Walk. It’s an Adirondack-style structure with two separate viewing platforms. Connected by a walkway, one platform is covered by a roof and the other by a trellis. The largest of the treehouses, it was designed by Forever Young Treehouses of Burlington, Vermont, co-founded by Bill Allen and Phil Trabulsy. Measuring 720-square-feet, it’s bigger than some urban apartments. Among its many intricate details is a handrail inspired by the Gothic Folly in Longwood’s recently opened Indoor Children’s Garden. Forever Young Treehouses is known for creating and promoting construction of treehouses that can be enjoyed by individuals with disabilitie…

