Longwood builds ‘nature’s castles’ in the gardens’ trees
Share this:
Share on Facebook (Opens in new window)Facebook
Share on X (Opens in new window)X
By Daily Local | dailylocal@dailylocal.com
PUBLISHED: April 24, 2008 at 4:23 AM EDT | UPDATED: August 20, 2021 at 5:17 AM EDT
Getting your Trinity Audio player ready...
Longwood Gardens has a delightful addition: three new tree houses nestled among the trees. Visitors can now literally find themselves up a tree, and it has all been done as beautifully. Last week there was a preview of theses newest attractions at Longwood. The press and others were invited for the speeches and ribbon cuttings, but the guests who had the most fun were the kids from the entire third grade at Pocopson Elementary School. All dressed in red T-shirts, they traipsed through the houses and added their happy chatter to the unveiling of the houses.The largest is called Canopy Cathedral, a two-story ornate house inspired by a Norwegian Stave Church. Designed by Peter Nelson and Jake Jacob of Treehouse Workshop Inc in Seattle, Wash., the 400-square foot house will have an intricate glass facade and hand carved fantastic creatures throughout.
It did not quite get finished for opening day, so the creatures were missing, as were the railings – which meant lots of Longwood employees were close by to grab you should a visitor misstep.
Near the Italian Water Garden, there is a view from the upper story that makes one want to move in for the summer. With cedar shakes on the roof and apparently a lot of cedar in the building, it has the heavenly smell of a new cedar closet. Carefully placed so it would not injure nearby trees, it is the catbird’s seat for just sitting and thinking things over.
Lookout Loft is a 720 square foot rustic Adirondack-style structure behind the Peirce House on Forest Walk. Designed to be handicapped accessible it is at ground level, although there is a bit of a hike over mulch, so you one drive a Longwood cart down to it. Designed by Forever Young Treehouses of Burlington, Vt., it has no walls, but has two joined viewing platforms in the woods with 13,000 pounds of salvaged steel holding it up – very unobtrusively. The pie wood used in the ornate handrails takes advantage of their matched natural contours for a lovely nature feeling. The wood is finished with a natural finish containing citrus oil and beeswax that smells lovely and is very attractive. Nestled closely amid the trees, a special pin foundation has concrete footings with extended steel rods that go between the tree roots and cause no damage.
The nearby third tree house called The Birdhouse, the tallest and the smallest, with 320 square feet, is made from salvaged wood from an old dairy barn. It rises 20 feet off the ground and gives a true bird’s eye view of Peirce’s woods, is also constructed of cedar, and has some wonderful benches made from slabs of wood. It has been carefully built over a large rotted out tree trunk, which has been kept so visitors will be able to peer deep inside the trunk to see what is living there. On opening day they were still waiting for a large piece of glass for viewing.
As the children climbed down from their view at the Birdhouse, they expressed their awe at the experience.
Rayna Miller, 8, said, “I’ve never been in one before.”
Dina Spyropoulos, 9, said, “I like the windows in the balcony. It felt like being in a birdhouse.”
Evangeline Gambardella, 9, said she has been in a tree-house before, but never one like this. “I thought it was cool,: she said.
There is supposed to also be an area where visitors can build their own idea of a tree house, but that was not up and running on the tree house opening day. The beginnings of Longwood Gardens commenced when Pierre duPont bought Pierce’s Woods of old majestic trees that was destined for the saw mill. He saved the woods and added the gardens we now enjoy.
As trees were the beginnings of Longwood Gardens, the addition of the tree houses is fitting. Longwood Director Paul Redman said, “Nature’s Castles is designed to celebrate trees and engage our guests in an exciting and unique way about the beauty and importance of trees.” These three new edifices are a gorgeous addition to Longwood.
During the opening day the rumor was going around that the tree houses were not permanent and would be torn down in November. Redman said it had not been determined how long the tree houses would be a part of Longwood, but they definitely would not be torn down in November.
