Something exciting will be in the air at Longwood Gardens – literally – when a spectacular display of grand treehouses opens today at the country’s premier horticultural showplace. Called “Nature’s Castles: The Treehouse Reimagined,” the display is part of Longwood’s Arbor Day weekend activities and continues through Nov. 23. Throughout spring, summer and fall, visitors of all ages can explore three unique, large-scale treehouses situated in picturesque parts of Longwood. The trio of tremendous treehouses showcases the creative work of the nation’s best treehouse designers.For young visitors, the display is a chance to climb up into a treehouse and get a birds-eye view of the Gardens below and beyond, while being reconnected with nature. For older visitors, the display rekindles memories of childhood, of carefree days spent in a rustic sanctuary high up in a tree. But these treehouses are definitely not the kind to be found in any suburban backyard. They’re magnificent structures, true castles in the air that show how Longwood has taken the treehouse to new heights.The first creation, Lookout Loft, is located in Longwood’s shady Forest Walk. It’s an Adirondack-style structure with two separate viewing platforms. Connected by a walkway, one platform is covered by a roof and the other by a trellis. The largest of the treehouses, it was designed by Forever Young Treehouses of Burlington, Vermont, co-founded by Bill Allen and Phil Trabulsy. Measuring 720-square-feet, it’s bigger than some urban apartments. Among its many intricate details is a handrail inspired by the Gothic Folly in Longwood’s recently opened Indoor Children’s Garden. Forever Young Treehouses is known for creating and promoting construction of treehouses that can be enjoyed by individuals with disabilities and special needs. A large ramp leading up to Lookout Loft makes it accessible to all.The second treehouse, Canopy Cathedral, overlooks Longwood’s beautiful Italian Water Garden. An amazing two-story, 400-square-foot tree dwelling, it was inspired by a Norwegian Stave Church, according to designers Peter Nelson and Jake Jacob of TreeHouse Workshop Inc. in Seattle, Washington. The towering tree structure features an intricate glass facade and hand-carved fantastical creatures throughout. A large deck allows visitors to enjoy a panoramic perch amid a grove of stately tulip poplars.Nelson, author of the leading books on treehouses, also designed the third treehouse. Called the Birdhouse, it’s the tallest of the three and stands majestically near the historic Peirce-du Pont house. Rising almost 20 feet above the ground, it measures an overall 320-square-feet. A series of steps leads visitors up to the top, where they find an outstanding view as well as informative placards.For example, they learn that lumber in the Birdhouse was recovered from an old dairy farm in Washington state. The floorboards came from a dismantled toothpaste factory in Toronto, Canada, and the 2-by-8-foot rafters were recovered from a dismantled warehouse in Vancouver, British Columbia. Also, the windows of tight-grained Douglas Fir came from trees felled in landslides or blown down in storms.At a press preview, Sharon Loving, head of Longwood’s horticulture department, stressed that the treehouses were designed and built with sensitivity to the environment. For instance, she said, recycled or reclaimed lumber was used in construction and no root systems were disturbed during construction. Although the houses are set high amid trees, preserving all aspects of the trees’ health and the surrounding landscape was of paramount importance to Longwood, she said. Therefore, the houses themselves do not rely on the trees for structural support. An example is Lookout Loft, which is held up by more than 1,300 pounds of steel. The treehouse display is a “natural” for Longwood, because trees are an important part of its history and mission. Longwood’s founder, Pierrre S. du Pont, purchased the Gardens’ original 202 acres to save an historic arboretum destined for the lumber mill. Many of those trees still stand in Longwood’s Peirce’s Park. “The ‘Nature’s Castles’ display is designed to celebrate trees and engage our guests in an exciting and unique way about the beauty and importance of trees,” said Longwood Gardens Director Paul Redman. “Young and old will be amazed by the size, artistry and intricate detail of these amazing structures. They’ll never think of treehouses in the same way after seeing these creations.” “Nature’s Castles” is just one of many exhibits, activities and events offered at Longwood to mark Arbor Day Weekend tomorrow and Sunday (April 25 & 27). Families can tour historic Peirce’s Park, meet the treehouse designers, enjoy Arbor Day storytelling, discover what it’s like to be an arborist and see exciting demonstrations of tree climbing and rigging by Longwood’s skilled arborists. The first 500 people each day of Arbor Day Weekend, 11 a.m.-3 p.m., will receive a sapling to take home.
