Built To Change: Measuring what actually predicts ERP success
Prosci
4 Mins
Updated: October 5, 2026
ERP leaders need metrics that show whether people can adopt the new system, use it as intended, and perform their work effectively. And with so many possible metrics, it’s not always clear which metrics to focus on.
In this episode of Built to Change, Emma de-la-Haye, Engagement Director for Prosci Europe, and Claire Guyot, Senior Principal Change Advisor, explain how to turn ERP measurement from a reporting exercise into a tool for better decisions and stronger results. They discuss the difference between activity metrics, such as training completion and communications sent, and steering metrics, such as whether employees can complete critical transactions independently. They also show how leaders can use those insights to respond with targeted training, coaching, manager support and other interventions.
Prosci Research shows high-quality measurement is essential to ERP success. Organizations with strong metrics are 2.5 times more likely to exceed expectations for their ERP transformations. Organizations with poor metrics succeed only 7% of the time, compared with 26% for organizations with no formal metrics. In other words, measuring the wrong things can do more harm than not measuring at all.
Which ERP metrics provide the clearest view of adoption?
Human factors of ROI, such as speed of adoption, ultimate utilization, and proficiency, are among the most useful ERP adoption metrics because they indicate whether people are actually using the new system effectively and contributing to the expected business results.
Speed of adoption shows how quickly people begin working effectively in the new environment.
Ultimate utilization shows whether the intended users use the new system and processes.
Proficiency shows how well people perform their work after adoption.
Why are activity metrics limited?
Activity metrics track things like training completion, communications sent, project milestones and support tickets—metrics that help teams manage delivery but provide little insight into whether people can use the new system effectively. For example, a completed training session confirms participation but does not show whether an employee can complete a purchase order, close the books or follow a new workflow without help.
Why can training completion give an incomplete picture?
Claire Guyot shares an example from an ERP deployment that used broad training across several functions. Attendance rates were high before go-live.
After go-live, employees struggled with role-specific tasks. Procurement teams needed different Knowledge and Ability from finance teams, and individual roles required more targeted instruction and practice.
The organization redesigned parts of the training after implementation.
The attendance data showed participation. Leaders also needed a measure of proficiency: whether employees could complete the work required in the new ERP.
×
Your ERP Will Go Live. Will It Deliver Value?
How does Prosci's ADKAR® Model support ERP measurement?
The Prosci ADKAR Model can help identify barriers that may slow adoption and reduce ERP performance. Tracking the ADKAR elements gives teams insight into where people need support:
Awareness shows whether people understand why the change is happening.
Desire shows whether they choose to support and participate in the change.
Knowledge shows whether they know how to change.
Ability shows whether they can apply that knowledge in their work.
Reinforcement shows whether the change is sustained over time.
In practice, teams can use an ADKAR Assessmentto measure progress across these elements for the people or groups most impacted by the ERP transformation. Individuals typically rate each element on a 1–5 scale, helping teams identify the ADKAR Barrier Point—the first element scoring 3 or below. This turns measurement into a diagnostic tool: rather than simply tracking employee sentiment, teams can pinpoint where adoption is at risk and target the right intervention. For example, a Desire barrier calls for a different response than a Knowledge or Ability barrier.
These measures complement adoption speed, ultimate utilization, and proficiency. For example, low Awareness may signal a need for clearer communication, low Knowledge may point to training gaps, and low Ability may call for more practice or coaching. This gives leaders more specific information about where to focus support.
What makes an ERP metric useful for leaders?
Useful metrics support decisions. A 95% training completion rate shows participation. If 50% of employees can complete a critical transaction without support, leaders have a clear measure of proficiency.
That result can prompt targeted training, coaching, manager support or process changes.
When selecting a metric, ask: What decision will this information support?
Specific targets also help. “90% of finance users can complete month-end closing independently within 30 days of go-live” defines the group, task, expected performance and timeframe.
Which ERP metrics should you focus on, and when?
The right metrics will shift as the rollout moves forward. Early on, leaders may focus on Awareness, Desire, stakeholder engagement and readiness. Closer to go-live, Knowledge, confidence and training effectiveness matter more. After launch, Ability, utilization and proficiency become more useful measures of performance.
Case in point: an energy organization adjusted its people metrics throughout the deployment, focusing on stakeholder engagement, communications, learning and adoption as priorities changed. As Claire Guyot explains, “It was not the number of metrics, but it was the fact that they were used to make decisions.”
How many ERP metrics should leaders track?
Claire Guyot recommends focusing on three to five metrics with a clear purpose, owner and threshold for action. Here’s why: A smaller set makes it easier to spot issues early and direct support where it will have the most impact. Project and change teams can track more operational measures, whereas leaders should focus on the metrics that show readiness, adoption and performance.
Measure what helps leaders act, not just what teams complete.
At the end of the day, the right metrics are the ones that give leaders a clear view of adoption and what needs attention. Low proficiency may call for role-based training, slow adoption may point to stronger manager support, and low Awareness may signal a communications or sponsorship gap.
The value comes from knowing what to act on, when. That gives leaders a better chance to correct course early and keep ERP adoption moving in the right direction.
Prosci helps organizations strengthen ERP measurement at every stage of implementation, so leaders can track adoption, spot gaps early and act on the signals that matter. If you want to improve how you measure ERP readiness, adoption and performance, talk to a Prosci consultant.
Prosci
As the global leader in change management, Prosci helps organizations turn complex change into something people understand—so they can act with confidence and deliver results. Built on more than 30 years of research, Prosci partners with enterprises to scale change, enable adoption, and realize outcomes across complex transformations, including ERP and AI. Our work brings clarity and structure to change, helping leaders move from strategy to action and ensure results endure. That’s what change done right looks like.
