The Gift is a seminal work by French sociologist and anthropologist Marcel Mauss. Published in 1925, the book explores the social and cultural significance of gift exchange across various societies, including those in Melanesia, Polynesia, and northwestern North America. 1

Mauss argued that gift exchange is not merely an economic transaction but a complex social phenomenon that creates and maintains relationships. He identified three key obligations inherent in gift exchange: the obligation to give, the obligation to receive, and the obligation to reciprocate. This system of giving, receiving, and returning gifts is deeply embedded in social structures, influencing prestige, social status, and even spiritual beliefs. The work highlights how these exchanges go beyond material value, serving to solidify social bonds and express existing relationships. 2

