In the expansive, mineral-rich terrains of Russia and Canada, mining is not a seasonal endeavor; it is a year-round operation that defies the extremities of nature. For mining operations in Yakutia, Siberia, or the Canadian Shield in Northern Quebec and Ontario, winter temperatures plummeting to -50°C and below are a operational reality. In these conditions, a used excavator is not just a piece of heavy equipment; it is the lifeblood of the mine’s productivity. However, the freezing cold is a relentless adversary to diesel engines and hydraulic systems. Viscosity increases exponentially, turning hydraulic fluid into a gel-like substance and making diesel fuel wax and “gel.” Starting a cold engine in these conditions without assistance is nearly impossible, and attempting to do so leads to catastrophic wear, reduced component life, and significant downtime.
...
Diesel engines rely on high compression heat to ignite the fuel. In sub-zero conditions, the engine block, cylinder head, and intake air are so cold that they absorb the heat of compression, preventing ignition. Glow plugs and intake heaters struggle to compensate when the entire block is a thermal sink. This leads to prolonged cranking, which drains batteries and washes cylinder walls with raw fuel, diluting the engine oil and reducing its lubricating properties.
...
A common misconception is that letting an excavator idle for an hour solves the problem. In reality, low-idle warm-ups in extreme cold are incredibly inefficient. The engine struggles to reach operating temperature, leading to wet stacking (unburnt fuel in the exhaust system), carbon buildup, and minimal heat transfer to the hydraulic fluid in the tank. This practice wastes fuel, increases emissions, and adds unnecessary hours to the engine meter wi…

