The copper mining sub-segment commands the largest market share, projected to capture 46.2% of industry share in 2026. Mongolia is exceptionally rich in copper, with some of the world’s largest active and relatively untapped deposits concentrated across the South Gobi and northern regions. The scale of Oyu Tolgoi further demonstrates this resource potential, with the operation targeting approximately 500,000 tonnes of annual copper production at full ramp-up, creating sustained requirements for large-scale mining and mineral-processing infrastructure.

The segment is further supported by Oyu Tolgoi and Erdenet, Mongolia’s two major copper complexes. Oyu Tolgoi’s underground ramp-up is increasing processing requirements, while Erdenet produced a record 574,000 tonnes of copper concentrate in 2025 and approved a USD 217 million investment program for 2026. Future demand is further supported by Oyu Tolgoi’s phased underground development through Panel 1, Panel 2 South, and Panel 2 North through 2036, alongside Erdenet’s ore-preparation modernization. These projects sustain recurring demand for crushers, SAG and ball mills, flotation circuits, thickeners, filtration systems, conveyors, and associated aftermarket components, reinforcing copper mining’s leading position. Based on mining type, the industry is segmented into:
