GT was originally developed by Glaser and Strauss (1967) as a new methodology defined as “the discovery of theory from data” (p. 1) and was a reaction at the same time to the dominant hypothetico-deductive use of “grand theories” in the social research of the 1960s. In contrast to only verifying theories by quantitative methods, they offered a set of qualitative methods for generating inductive theories from data. After their collaboration, the two originators took differ ent career paths and by the early1990s, two distinct versions of GT had emerged – Glaserian GT (Glaser, 1978, 1992, 1998) and Straussian GT, which in large part Strauss developed in collaboration with Corbin (Strauss, 1987; Strauss & Corbin, 1990, 1998). A third version called constructivist GT was developed by Charmaz (2000, 2006, 2008, 2009) and others (Bryant, 2002; Mills, Bonner, & Francis, 2006a; Mills, Bonner, & Francis, 2006b). Other examples of later developments in GT methodology are Dey’s (1999) version with a elaborated view on categoriza tion, process, causality, and structure/agency in GT, and Clarke’s (2005) postmodern version called situational analysis.

