As Glaser and Strauss continued to mature GT, their progression precipitated professional and methodological divergence. In the 1970's and 80's Glaser and Strauss each wrote a further exposition of GT, but published their books separately rather than collaboratively (Glaser, 1978; Strauss, 1987) . By 1990 Strauss had forged an academic alliance with Juliet Corbin and together they refined particular features of the original (Classic) GT. Strauss and Corbin revised the original precept of a natural emergence of a theory from data, to be discovered by the researcher. Instead, they devised a highly analytical and prescriptive framework for coding, designed to deduce theory from data systematically (there is not the scope in this article to explore this). This rigorous and meticulous coding framework was underlined by the philosophy of pragmatism and symbolic interactionism. Although there was certainly a nuance of this philosophical inclination embedded within the original configuration of GT (due to Strauss' influence), it was patently more significant and seminal in Strauss and Corbin's reconfiguration of GT. Strauss and Corbin also challenged the tenet of abstaining from literature prior to embarking on the study, highlighting the difference between an "open mind" versus an "empty mind" (Jones & Alony, 2011, p. 99) . Consequently, this transformation of the original tenets of GT fashioned the alternative Straussian GT which Strauss and Corbin assembled in their book, Basics of Qualitative Research: Grounded Theory Procedures and Techniques (1990) .

