GT is a research approach concerned with the generation of theory from data that is systematically gathered and analysed via rigorous methods (Noble and Mitchell 2016) . There are three styles of GT, and while there are some commonalities in the styles, the approach and rigour of certain criteria within each style differ (Evans 2013) . Despite sharing a number of the original methodological techniques, Classic, Straussian, and Constructivist GT are separate and distinct research methodologies (Kenny and Fourie 2015) , each distinguished by specific philosophical frameworks and differing methodological processes (Table 1 ) (Connor et al. 2023) . Classic GT proposes to 'discover' a theory through analysis of the data (Glaser and Strauss 1967) ; Staussian GT uses data analysis to 'create' theory (Corbin and Strauss 2015) and Constructivist GT employs an adaptable coding process to 'construct theory' (Charmaz 2024) .

