The purpose of this study is to investigate which form of assessment (performance-based measures or rating scales) should be given more weight in making an ADHD diagnosis. Often these measures do not correlate, and clinicians are faced with conflicting protocols. This study will assess correlations between performance-based assessments and rating scales. Specifically, this study will correlate working memory indexes on the WISC-V with working memory indexes on the BRIEF2. The correlation between inhibition scores on the NEPSY-II with inhibition indexes from the BRIEF2 will also be examined. This study will assess which type of measure contributes greatest to an ADHD score, measured by two DSM symptom scales on the Conners 3. This study will also assess the incremental validity of neuropsychological tests with rating scales to improve sensitivity and specificity in an ADHD diagnosis.

