See Appendix 1 for synthesis of reviewed articles. Overarchingly the literature highlighted that while there was a widely accepted correlation between domains of executive function deficits (specifically working memory and processing speed), and ADHD symptom expression (Faedda et al., Citation 2021; Swanson, Citation 2003; Tallberg et al., Citation 2021; Zografou & Drigas, Citation 2022) it is clinically challenging to isolate those deficits from either sluggish or low cognitive ability or a co-occurring specific learning difficulty.

It is also important to note that there is evidence that the WISC-V has limited sensitivity to executive functioning deficits in ADHD with an associated risk of inaccurate diagnosis in the form of missed identification. This can be attributed to multiple factors. Cognitive assessments are completed in quiet, structured, 1:1 environments that, while essential to the manualised administration of the test, do not reflect daily environments that children are subject to including conflicting attentional demands. This may not produce scores truly indicative of the child’s ability (Torres van Grinsven, Citation 2022). Further limitations include the heterogeneity in executive functioning difficulties amongst children with ADHD and limitations of the WISC-V in assessing the components of executive functioning other than working memory such as inhibitory control and set-shifting (Kofler et al., Citation 2019). These factors highlight the limitations of the WISC-V as it is designed to measure only one of the three primary executive functions in ADHD (working memory, but not inhibitory control or set-shifting) (Kofler et al., Citation 2019).
