[3] The web service Wilma as an actant in school life – student perspective (2020-10-30T00:00:00.000Z)
URL: https://doi.org/10.4324/9781003024705-5
# The web service Wilma as an actant in school life – student perspective
...
Digital technologies are nowadays an integral part of students’ school life, and they have also affected the ways schools and homes cooperate. In Finland, a web service titled ‘Wilma’ is widely used in compulsory education and in upper secondary schools as a communication and an administration system. Through it, students can, for example, register for courses, check their grades, read announcements, get feedback and communicate with teachers. Teachers can grade their students, register absences and update students’ personal information, and communicate with students and their guardians. Furthermore, guardians can communicate with their children’s teachers and follow their children’s progress at school. In this chapter, the web service Wilma is studied through the interviews of secondary and upper secondary school students. Actor-network theory is utilised to understand how the service is constructed and deconstructed through the interaction of human and non-human elements, both enabling and challenging students’ everyday life at school.

