Power Distance 

South Korea is a slightly more hierarchical society than Finland. This may influence the way business is done in the country, as well as the expectations South Korean employees hold towards a company’s culture. A company located in Finland with its lower hierarchical structures is used to allowing its employees to interact with managers on somewhat equal footing. For instance, it may be common to address higher-ups with their first names. In contrast, more hierarchical business cultures are common that, for example, include an intricate system of reporting to higher-ups within the country.
...
The gap between Finland and South Korea in terms of their degree of individualism is significant, with the former being considered a fairly individualistic society and the latter a collectivistic one. When it comes to business, this may be reflected in the employee’s mindset towards their work and responsibilities. For instance, while a Finnish employee may choose to miss a deadline as they are ill, a South Korean worker might meet that deadline despite their illness because they do not want to let down their manager who entrusted them with said task.

 More generally speaking, this dimension affects the way an individual employee sees themselves within the company – either as one individual striving to be successful (Finland) within a company or as part of the entity that is the company (South Korea). In practice, managers must be aware of such differences and clearly establish and communicate a workplace culture meeting either side of the spectrum or a compromise in between.
