This thesis considers the role that Achilles plays throughout the Iliad as an agent of uncertainty and disruptive force within the traditional story of the Trojan War. Throughout the Iliad, characters regularly make claims about future events, either in ignorance or, if they are divine, with authoritative foreknowledge. I consider a series of claims that Achilles makes about future events, particularly his claim that Troy will never fall (9.417-420), in the context of the epic tradition and the poet’s narrative limitations. I argue that, while the shape of the poet’s story is limited by his audience’s expectations and familiarity with tradition, the poet is able to take advantage of Achilles’s unusual positioning within the poem in order to develop an aura of uncertainty and instability within a familiar storyline. In Part I, I begin with an analysis of Achilles’s unique position, including his connection to the gods and his awareness of his own fate in contrast to other mortal characters, and argue that his divinity and foreknowledge make him an authoritative source of future predictions. Part II considers the poet’s limitations and the other ways he creates uncertainty within the Iliad, including through reversal clauses and false predictions of future events. These techniques create moments of brief uncertainty, but Achilles disrupts broader swaths of the poem through his quarrel with Agamemnon and his vengeance against Hector. In Part III, I turn to the site of greatest uncertainty in the Iliad: Achilles’s aristeia. Book 20 features repeated scenes where the gods are forced to intervene in order to correct the fates of various characters during or after their encounters with Achilles. This threatened violation of the traditional fall of Troy gains legitimacy becaus…

