Thetis here is referring to Achilles’ grief over Agamemnon’s insult and seems to have accepted her son’s imminent death. Her very presence in Hephaistos’ workshop is intended to facilitate Achilles' return to battle with a new armor, fashioned by the god metallurgist following Patroklos’ death at the hands of Hek-tor. Achilles’ possible or attempted immortality is not hinted at anywhere. On the contrary, Thetis’ acceptance of her son’s mortality in the Iliad is in agreement with her constant concern to keep him out of harm’s way, which also constitutes her defining characteristic in the epic tradition. Not only is she concerned, as every mother would be, about the dangers of war, but she also knows (1.417-418, XVIII.95-96), 23 although it is not very clear to us how, that Achilles will die in Troy. Moreover, there is Kalchas' prophecy that Troy cannot be taken without Achilles. Thus, knowing that Achilles will die if he goes to war (npoarduia orr oar OTpatauopEvov aurov aTioAaoOai, Apollod.3.174.3), she sends him disguised as a girl to the court of king Lycornedes in Skyros. 24 The element of foreknowledge of death, which the Iliad so brilliantly (and inextricably) intertwines with Achilles’ presence at Troy, also governs Thetis’ agency in respect to her sori’s fate. 25 The pattern of protecting Achilles from enlistment is augmented with the th…
