[8] Cypria — Grokipedia
URL: https://grokipedia.com/page/Cypria
No complete manuscripts of the Cypria survive from antiquity or the medieval period. [11] The poem is preserved primarily through an ancient summary attributed to Proclus in his Chrestomathy (5th century AD), which outlines its structure in eleven books and key narrative elements, and through marginal scholia commenting on Homer's Iliad. [14] This summary of the Cypria survives independently in later copies, as the relevant folium is mutilated in the primary witness for the Epic Cycle summaries, the 10th-century Venetus A manuscript of the Iliad. [15] Additional transmission occurs via excerpts in the Venetus A, a Byzantine deluxe codex containing scholia with quotations and references to the Cypria, and in compilations such as Photius's Bibliotheca (9th century AD), which cites and preserves portions of Proclus's Chrestomathy. [16] These medieval sources reflect a selective copying tradition focused on integrating Cyclic material into Homeric exegesis rather than independent preservation. [17] The Cypria was largely lost during the Middle Ages due to the predominant scholarly and cultural emphasis on Homer's canonical Iliad and Odyssey, which overshadowed the less central Epic Cycle poems and led to reduced manuscript production for the latter. [18] Fragments endured indirectly through quotations in ancient authors such as Athenaeus (2nd–3rd century AD) and the Library of Apollodorus (1st–2nd century AD), which drew upon Cyclic narratives for mythological accounts. [4] Approximately twenty-seven such fragments are known, varying from brief lines to testimonia. [11] ... efforts to reconstruct the ... 9th-century scholarly ... a (18 ... testimonia, summaries, and quotations into a ... corpus for analysis. [19]
