The most influential mid-twenty-century work for the study of the Cypria 
is Kullmann’s Die Quellen der Ilias (1960), which became a cornerstone of 
what had already been coined (Kakridis 1944) as “Neoanalysis”. Neoanalysis, 
in its broadest sense, seeks to explain Homeric narrative choices—especially 
anomalous or unmotivated features—by reference to other mythic narratives 
belonging to pre-Homeric epics that Homer’s poem presupposes, reworks, or 
suppresses. In this framework, the Cypria (together with other Cyclic poems) 
becomes less a derivative afterpiece and more a crucial witness to the larger 
Trojan War epic tradition against which the Iliad defines itself. 
In particular, the Iliad’s consistent use of flashbacks in Books 2–7 to recall 
mythic episodes that (a) precede the poem’s narrative and (b) later appear in 
the post-Homeric Cypria indicates (for Neoanalysis) that the Iliad is drawing 
on an earlier, pre-Homeric epic tradition that already contained these events, 
rather than that the poet of the Cypria retroactively assembled them from scat 
tered allusions in the Iliad. 
