so they can be used postoperatively if needed. The sinus node often recovers, even in patients who require a permanent pacemaker; only 30% to 40% of patients remain dependent on a pacemaker during long-term follow-up.8No class I recommendations exist for medications in the management of bradycardia. Atropine, however, is reasonable and is a common first-line medication for treating symptomatic bradycardia secondary to sinus node dysfunction.1 Atropine is a parasympatholytic medication that increases sinus node automaticity and conduction through the sinoatrial node. It shortens the sinus cycle, the atrial refractory period, and the time required for the sinus node to recover.9 The typical dose of atropine for the treatment of bradycardia is 0.5 to 2.0 mg. Lower doses of atropine can cause the heart rate to decrease.10 Sympathomimetic drugs such as dopamine or epinephrine can be used as an alternative to atropine in patients who are at low risk for coronary ischemia.1 Atropine is not recommended for treating bradycardia in patients who have received a heart transplant unless evidence shows reinnervation of the autonomic nervous system,1 because in such patients atropine may cause a paradoxical high-grade AV block or sinus arrest, leading to asystole.11, 12 Reinnervation of the autonomic nervous system is possible after transplantation but may occur at different rates between the sympathetic and parasympathetic systems and may be affected by surgical technique.13Temporary or permanent pacing is not recommended in all patients with sinus node dysfunction or bradycardia. Patients who have no or infrequent symptoms, who are hemodynamically stable, or whose bradyarrhythmia occurs only during sleep do not meet the criteria for pacing therapy.1 Pacing is indicated when a patie…
