For this new adventure, I partnered with my padawan Grégor Arzouyan, and we started diving into the world of GitHub Actions. Our goal was to replace an old component in our CI/CD system called Codeship. Codeship was used to do Pull Request checks on every commit to ensure the lint and build of the code, plus unit tests, happened successfully. Like Jenkins, Codeship had scalability issues because we could not reuse logic. For each of 600 repositories, we had to duplicate the steps and environment configuration, and setting up a new repository or updating the current ones was an annoying and repetitive task.
...
So we picked EKS to orchestrate our computing resources, ... enter to scale those resources quickly, and Actions Runner controller (ARC) to operate the GitHub Actions runners. With this toolset, we have a solution that is auto-scalable. Every time a GitHub Actions Workflow needs a runner, ARC spawns a new runner, and if we don’t have a node ready, Karpenter handles that. Having runners up and processing workflows in less than 1 minute was terrific. And the amazing part was that everything was controlled using Kubernetes resources and containers
...
The true power of GitHub Actions started to reveal itself to us when we began to roll out the solution to replace Codeship. In the past, we had to manage an extra platform with all the work to manage users, and set up Codeship for each repository; now it is integrated into our most-used tool, GitHub. All the repository-specific scripts and projects that need to be configured manually now are reusable workflows that could be “installed” automatically. And the ability to manage secrets in one central place, instead of juggling them across multiple projects, was a massive win, with the big plus that this time we already h…

