In the last article of Pipedrive’s CI/CD Switch, we saw that Pipedrive had issues with Jenkins, so we created a custom CI engine to address those issues. However, in the end, the solution proved to be inadequate. We followed the investigation effort led by Gregor and me to implement a self-hosting solution for GitHub Actions using EKS, Karpenter, and ARC. We successfully migrated 600 repositories to GitHub Actions using reusable workflows. Additionally, we did research to understand the potential form of the new CI/CD after experimenting with several tools, including Tekton and Spinnaker. To finally understand that the magical combo will be GitHub Actions plus ArgoCD.

In this part, I will guide you in our journey to convert that POC into a production-ready solution, the marathon that was to migrate 700 services using automation, and where, at some time, the developers start to be independent and do it alone, and the cracks we got when bots overloaded the system.
